/* blocks.lps — the example that separates the two searches (§I.7.4).

   Seven blocks in one tower, to be rebuilt in the reverse order. The optimal
   plan is seven moves — each block is picked up exactly once — and that is
   the length at which breadth-first search stops being a strategy: the
   branching factor grows as blocks come free, so the frontier at depth seven
   is beyond anything worth waiting for.

   Greedy best-first on the delete-relaxation heuristic solves it at once,
   because the relaxation — "never mind that moving a block un-clears the one
   underneath" — is the simplification blocks world was built to punish by hand
   and is easy for by machine.

   Measured here, same machine, same program:

     ./lps run examples/blocks.lps --search greedy      0.4 s
     ./lps run examples/blocks.lps                      5.3 s   (auto)
     ./lps run examples/blocks.lps --search bfs        25.5 s

   and the gap is exponential in the number of blocks, not constant: at five
   blocks BFS still wins on wall time, at seven it is sixty times slower, and
   at nine it does not come back. `auto` pays the budgeted BFS first and then
   switches, which is the price of not having to know in advance.

   The declarative goat (examples/goat_declarative.pl) is the other side of the
   same coin: BFS finishes there in a few hundred nodes and returns the classic
   seven crossings, which is the *shortest* answer and not merely an answer.
   That is why it pins `search(bfs)` and this one does not.
*/

:- lps_engine(planning, [search(auto), horizon(14), max_concurrency(1)]).

maxTime(16).

fluents  on(_, _).
actions  move(_, _, _).

initially on(a, b), on(b, c), on(c, d), on(d, e), on(e, f), on(f, g),
	  on(g, table).

%  One action, one causal law: a block moves from where it is to somewhere
%  else. Everything else about the domain is a constraint.
move(Block, From, To) updates From to To in on(Block, From).

/*  `clear` and `covered` are *intensional* fluents (§8 of docs/lps_summary.md):
    computed from `on/2` rather than maintained alongside it. Saying it once
    here is worth more than three causal laws that have to agree with each
    other, and it is why this program has one action rather than four.
*/
covered(X) at T if on(_, X) at T.
clear(X)   at T if not covered(X) at T.
clear(table) at _.

%  Applicability. The times are explicit and pinned to the *start* of the
%  action, because an untimed fluent beside an action in a denial anchors to
%  the interval — which would make these read as constraints on the state the
%  move produces, i.e. as prospective constraints.
false move(Block, _, _)  from T1 to _, not clear(Block) at T1.
false move(_, _, To)     from T1 to _, not clear(To) at T1.
false move(Block, From, _) from T1 to _, not on(Block, From) at T1.
false move(Block, _, Block).
false move(Block, From, From).

achieve on(g, f), on(f, e), on(e, d), on(d, c), on(c, b), on(b, a).
